# name: my-step
# desc: One line describing what this step does (shown by --list)
# default: on
# ask: Optional question — add this line only when the step is a real
#      per-project choice that must never be decided silently.
#
# Copy this file to modules/NN-my-step.sh (NN = two digits deciding run order)
# and it is picked up automatically — nothing else needs registering.
#
# The module runs with the project root as the working directory and with these
# already available (see lib/common.sh):
#
#   PROJECT_DIR  absolute project root
#   SKILL_DIR    this skill's directory
#   COMPOSER_CMD / ARTISAN_CMD / PHP_CMD    Sail-aware tool runners — always call them as
#                               `run $COMPOSER_CMD ...`, never bare `composer`
#   run <cmd>            execute, or just print under --dry-run
#   step / info / warn / die     output helpers
#   asset <file>                 path to a file in assets/
#   install_asset <src> <dest>   copy a config file in, backing up a differing one
#   set_env_var <file> <K> <V>   set a key in .env / .env.example
#   merge_composer_scripts <f>   merge a JSON script map into composer.json
#   artisan_has <command>        true when artisan exposes that command
#   set_state PROJECT_DIR <p>    tell the runner the project moved
#
# One trap worth knowing: when the run goes through Sail, $PHP_CMD and
# $ARTISAN_CMD execute inside a container that mounts only the project
# directory. A path under $SKILL_DIR does not exist there, so a skill asset has
# to be staged in the project before being handed to them — see
# merge_composer_scripts in lib/common.sh. Host-side helpers (cp, bash reads,
# install_asset) are unaffected.
#
# Keep the module idempotent: it should be safe to run twice on the same
# project. Failing modules do not abort the run — the runner reports them at
# the end — so it is fine to `die` when a precondition is genuinely missing.

info "doing the thing..."
run $COMPOSER_CMD require --dev --no-interaction some/package
